d3d10umd: small indexable temp arrays as registers, indexed by selects

An access to an indexable temp array (x#[] in Direct3D 10 shaders) through an index held in a
register reached the host as a dynamically indexed array, which a GPU compiler may keep in
scratch memory. Arrays of up to 16 elements are now plain registers: a read gathers the element
with compare-and-select before the instruction, a write is scattered to the elements after it.
The geometry shader of WinSAT's second geometry test ran ten times as fast on an Intel UHD 630
host (68 to 704 frames a second). RIPPLE_INDEXED_TEMPS keeps the arrays.

